Subject: winemac.drv: correct a borderless window placed at the work-area origin (macOS)

rcWork and rcMonitor share an origin on Windows -- the taskbar is at the bottom
-- but not on macOS, where the menu bar is at the top. Measured in a bottle:

    rcMonitor  0,0   1470x956
    rcWork     0,33  1470x923

So an app that asks for the work-area origin while sizing to the full monitor is
self-consistent only on Windows, and only because of where the taskbar happens
to be. Here the same code lands one menu-bar height too low: the menu bar stays
visible across the top and the same number of pixels hang off the bottom.
Fullscreen game windows do this (Unity's -popupwindow, for instance).

Snap exactly that case: a borderless window the size of a display whose top edge
was aligned to that display's work area. An earlier version matched on
"borderless, within a pixel of a display's size, centre inside it, not already
at the origin", which also dragged back a window an app had deliberately placed
elsewhere -- tests/window-snap-test.c covers that case.

Having moved the NSWindow somewhere Wine did not ask for, Wine has to be told,
or its rects keep the pre-snap origin and every click lands offset by the menu
bar. The frame-changed event that normally does this is emitted only when the
window is ordered in and only when the frame actually changed; initial
positioning runs before show_window(), and a repeat SetWindowPos with the same
rect compares equal once the NSWindow already sits at the snapped origin. So the
event is forced whenever a snap happened.

Titled windows are left alone: one whose content happened to be display-sized
would have its title bar pushed under the menu bar. They are separately subject
to AppKit's constrainFrameRect:toScreen:, which pins a titled window's top a
couple of points below the work area regardless of what was asked for -- Wine
reports the constrained position faithfully, which is correct and not something
this patch should fight.

diff --git a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m
index 7d6d704436c..c2568f54276 100644
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m
@@ -1956,6 +1956,38 @@ - (void) setFrameFromWine:(NSRect)contentRect
            (left, bottom) in a bottom-up space. */
         [[WineApplicationController sharedController] flipRect:&contentRect];
 
+        BOOL snapped = FALSE;
+
+        /* rcWork and rcMonitor share an origin on Windows -- the taskbar is at
+           the bottom -- but not on macOS, where the menu bar is at the top.  An
+           app that asks for the work-area origin while sizing to the full
+           monitor is therefore self-consistent only on Windows; here it lands
+           one menu-bar height too low, leaving the menu bar visible and hanging
+           the same number of pixels off the bottom.
+
+           Correct exactly that: a window the size of a display whose top edge
+           was aligned to that display's work area.  Matching on "borderless and
+           display-sized and not at the origin" instead would also drag back a
+           window an app deliberately placed elsewhere.
+
+           Still borderless-only: a titled window whose content happened to be
+           display-sized would have its title bar pushed under the menu bar. */
+        if (!([self styleMask] & NSWindowStyleMaskTitled) && !NSIsEmptyRect(contentRect))
+        {
+            for (NSScreen* snapScreen in [NSScreen screens])
+            {
+                NSRect sf = [snapScreen frame], vf = [snapScreen visibleFrame];
+
+                if (!NSEqualSizes(contentRect.size, sf.size)) continue;
+                if (fabs(NSMaxY(contentRect) - NSMaxY(vf)) > 1.0) continue;
+                if (NSEqualPoints(contentRect.origin, sf.origin)) continue;
+
+                contentRect.origin = sf.origin;
+                snapped = TRUE;
+                break;
+            }
+        }
+
         /* The back end is establishing a new window size and position.  It's
            not interested in any stale events regarding those that may be sitting
            in the queue. */
@@ -1965,6 +1997,7 @@ - (void) setFrameFromWine:(NSRect)contentRect
         if (!NSIsEmptyRect(contentRect))
         {
             NSRect frame, oldFrame;
+            BOOL frameChanged = FALSE;
 
             oldFrame = self.wine_fractionalFrame;
             frame = [self frameRectForContentRect:contentRect];
@@ -2016,13 +2049,21 @@ - (void) setFrameFromWine:(NSRect)contentRect
 
                 [self updateFullscreen];
 
-                if ([self isOrderedIn])
-                {
-                    /* In case Cocoa adjusted the frame we tried to set, generate a frame-changed
-                       event.  The back end will ignore it if nothing actually changed. */
-                    [self windowDidResize:nil skipSizeMove:TRUE];
-                }
+                frameChanged = TRUE;
             }
+
+            /* In case Cocoa adjusted the frame we tried to set, generate a frame-changed
+               event.  The back end will ignore it if nothing actually changed.
+
+               Also unconditionally after a snap: that moved the NSWindow somewhere
+               Wine did not ask for, and Wine has to be told or every click lands
+               offset by the menu-bar height.  Neither of the two conditions above
+               covers it -- initial positioning runs before show_window(), so
+               isOrderedIn is false, and once the NSWindow sits at the snapped
+               origin a repeat SetWindowPos with the same rect compares equal to
+               oldFrame, so the desync would never be repaired. */
+            if ((frameChanged && [self isOrderedIn]) || snapped)
+                [self windowDidResize:nil skipSizeMove:TRUE];
         }
     }
 
